1
00:00:06,465 --> 00:00:08,383
<i>Previously on</i> Reacher...

2
00:00:08,384 --> 00:00:09,384
A woman kills herself on the same train

3
00:00:09,385 --> 00:00:12,178
<i>as the chief of staff
of a potential presidential candidate?</i>

4
00:00:12,179 --> 00:00:13,680
CIA starts kidnapping people over it?

5
00:00:13,681 --> 00:00:16,224
- [shouting, grunting]
- That's a story.

6
00:00:16,225 --> 00:00:17,934
I don't know where Ben is,
but I knew that girl--

7
00:00:17,935 --> 00:00:19,310
the one he left with the other night.

8
00:00:19,311 --> 00:00:21,062
Yeah, real name's Mary Ellen Daniels.

9
00:00:21,063 --> 00:00:22,605
Do you have an address?

10
00:00:22,606 --> 00:00:23,648
[propulsive music playing]

11
00:00:23,649 --> 00:00:25,483
<i>Whoever did this weren't amateurs.</i>

12
00:00:25,484 --> 00:00:26,985
Found the self-checkout's car.

13
00:00:26,986 --> 00:00:28,903
[Russell] <i>Any idea why
she'd ditch a perfectly good car,</i>

14
00:00:28,904 --> 00:00:30,114
<i>take the subway?</i>

15
00:00:31,073 --> 00:00:32,949
[Jacob] <i>It's a work badge.</i>

16
00:00:32,950 --> 00:00:33,908
[beeps]

17
00:00:33,909 --> 00:00:36,828
[Russell] <i>Name: Anna Merrick.
Work address and</i>

18
00:00:36,829 --> 00:00:38,329
level eight clearance.

19
00:00:38,330 --> 00:00:39,498
[Tamara] <i>Well, at least we know
where we're going to next.</i>

20
00:00:40,958 --> 00:00:42,459
What the hell was Anna doing here?

21
00:00:42,460 --> 00:00:44,669
[Lila] <i>Putra and his soldiers</i>

22
00:00:44,670 --> 00:00:47,046
were trained by
an American Delta Force specialist.

23
00:00:47,047 --> 00:00:49,215
- John Sampson.
- [Lila] That drive has the proof

24
00:00:49,216 --> 00:00:50,633
connecting Sampson to all of this.

25
00:00:50,634 --> 00:00:53,928
I have never tortured or killed civilians.

26
00:00:53,929 --> 00:00:56,014
- What about your chief of staff?
- What about him?

27
00:00:56,015 --> 00:00:57,098
[Reacher] <i>Why were you on the subway</i>

28
00:00:57,099 --> 00:00:59,184
with Anna Merrick the night she died?

29
00:00:59,185 --> 00:01:02,395
- I'm sorry, John.
- [John] Nolan.

30
00:01:02,396 --> 00:01:03,939
- Nolan. Nolan.
- No!

31
00:01:06,025 --> 00:01:08,027
[indistinct police radio chatter]

32
00:01:11,030 --> 00:01:13,032
{\an8}[ominous music playing]

33
00:01:18,913 --> 00:01:20,915
[camera shutter clicking]

34
00:01:25,544 --> 00:01:28,671
And had Mr. Cahill
exhibited any signs of distress?

35
00:01:28,672 --> 00:01:31,132
- No.
- Um, any particular matters

36
00:01:31,133 --> 00:01:34,719
weighing on him-- romantic
or financial-- anything like that?

37
00:01:34,720 --> 00:01:36,262
Not to my knowledge.

38
00:01:36,263 --> 00:01:39,224
Did he leave a note,
a text message, maybe an email?

39
00:01:39,225 --> 00:01:41,935
Look, Detective, like,
I-I appreciate your thoroughness,

40
00:01:41,936 --> 00:01:44,521
but he didn't leave anything. He displayed

41
00:01:44,522 --> 00:01:46,148
no red flags, and right now, I'm...

42
00:01:49,318 --> 00:01:52,779
If you could excuse us, I'm just trying
to deal with the loss of my friend.

43
00:01:52,780 --> 00:01:55,406
Understood, Congressman.

44
00:01:55,407 --> 00:01:56,991
If I have any more questions,
I'll reach out

45
00:01:56,992 --> 00:01:58,952
- to your office.
- Thank you.

46
00:01:58,953 --> 00:02:01,287
How do I get a hold of you, Mr. Reacher?

47
00:02:01,288 --> 00:02:02,957
You can't.

48
00:02:05,668 --> 00:02:09,003
My office can pass along any inquiries
you have for Mr. Reacher.

49
00:02:09,004 --> 00:02:10,797
Okay, yeah. Good enough.

50
00:02:10,798 --> 00:02:13,300
Um, my condolences.

51
00:02:15,386 --> 00:02:16,970
Front desk just called, sir.

52
00:02:16,971 --> 00:02:19,973
News crews are arriving.
We got to get you someplace else.

53
00:02:19,974 --> 00:02:23,393
Well, it's not a secret
that I was staying at the hotel.

54
00:02:23,394 --> 00:02:24,811
Or that Nolan worked for me.

55
00:02:24,812 --> 00:02:27,397
A visual of you at the scene
of a suicide is not good for--

56
00:02:27,398 --> 00:02:29,357
Oh, for fuck's sake, Springfield.

57
00:02:29,358 --> 00:02:31,484
I served with that man for 11 years,

58
00:02:31,485 --> 00:02:33,570
and I'm not gonna
leave him in a parking lot

59
00:02:33,571 --> 00:02:34,612
underneath a tarp.

60
00:02:34,613 --> 00:02:37,407
John, there's nothing
you can do for him now.

61
00:02:37,408 --> 00:02:39,492
The press is gonna want the quotes,

62
00:02:39,493 --> 00:02:41,411
and Nolan would've been
the first one to tell you

63
00:02:41,412 --> 00:02:44,163
that you are in no state
to go on the record right now.

64
00:02:44,164 --> 00:02:46,624
This is no different
than your military training.

65
00:02:46,625 --> 00:02:49,795
Sometimes you have to just
fall back and regroup.

66
00:02:52,298 --> 00:02:54,300
[somber music playing]

67
00:02:55,968 --> 00:02:57,510
I could use a beer.

68
00:02:57,511 --> 00:02:59,053
It's 9:00 a.m.

69
00:02:59,054 --> 00:03:00,472
I know a third shift bar.

70
00:03:18,073 --> 00:03:19,283
Thanks.

71
00:03:20,618 --> 00:03:21,618
Thank you.

72
00:03:21,619 --> 00:03:23,286
I didn't know
what kind of sweetener you liked,

73
00:03:23,287 --> 00:03:26,373
so I got, uh, sugar,
Splenda and Sweet'N Low.

74
00:03:27,374 --> 00:03:28,666
Sugar's fine, thanks.

75
00:03:28,667 --> 00:03:31,921
Okay, for the future, sugar it is.

76
00:03:33,714 --> 00:03:35,548
Oh, shit. Listen to what I just found.

77
00:03:35,549 --> 00:03:37,342
Last year, Congress passed

78
00:03:37,343 --> 00:03:39,427
a really boring, bipartisan
deficit reduction plan.

79
00:03:39,428 --> 00:03:40,762
It's a million little paper cuts

80
00:03:40,763 --> 00:03:43,181
to hopefully bleed out
our massive national debt.

81
00:03:43,182 --> 00:03:44,891
It's window dressing
to convince the populace

82
00:03:44,892 --> 00:03:46,809
our politicians
are actually doing something.

83
00:03:46,810 --> 00:03:48,353
- Focus.
- Right, sorry.

84
00:03:48,354 --> 00:03:50,813
Anyway, one of the paper cuts
was a government-wide

85
00:03:50,814 --> 00:03:53,358
move away from costly
document storage facilities,

86
00:03:53,359 --> 00:03:55,526
massive warehouses
that stored decades-old records.

87
00:03:55,527 --> 00:03:57,028
Between rental fees, maintenance,

88
00:03:57,029 --> 00:03:58,571
they were really expensive.

89
00:03:58,572 --> 00:04:01,658
Now, per the new law,
most documents over ten years old

90
00:04:01,659 --> 00:04:04,077
are to be digitized,
uploaded to the secure server,

91
00:04:04,078 --> 00:04:05,870
and then the paper copies destroyed.

92
00:04:05,871 --> 00:04:08,332
This has to be where they're doing it.

93
00:04:10,501 --> 00:04:11,542
You guessing or you know?

94
00:04:11,543 --> 00:04:13,670
Well, the government
doesn't exactly publicize the addresses

95
00:04:13,671 --> 00:04:15,755
of their secret
document destruction depots,

96
00:04:15,756 --> 00:04:18,132
but I've studied enough
of these federally-owned buildings

97
00:04:18,133 --> 00:04:20,218
to know that that smokestack

98
00:04:20,219 --> 00:04:23,554
is a ventilator
for a commercial-grade incinerator.

99
00:04:23,555 --> 00:04:25,056
When the government
needs something important

100
00:04:25,057 --> 00:04:26,474
go away, they burn it.

101
00:04:26,475 --> 00:04:28,726
That's why nobody's ever seen
the original Warren Commission Report.

102
00:04:28,727 --> 00:04:29,727
Burnt up.

103
00:04:29,728 --> 00:04:31,229
Why not just shred 'em?

104
00:04:31,230 --> 00:04:33,440
Haven't you ever seen <i>Argo?</i>
You can tape that shit back together.

105
00:04:34,441 --> 00:04:36,150
We got to get in there.

106
00:04:36,151 --> 00:04:37,610
We got to see what Anna had access to.

107
00:04:37,611 --> 00:04:40,238
- And stole.
- Hey, we don't know that.

108
00:04:40,239 --> 00:04:42,573
Man, I'm not trying
to take a shot at your sister,

109
00:04:42,574 --> 00:04:45,493
but she was working at
a top-clearance government building

110
00:04:45,494 --> 00:04:47,537
and she left with
a thumb drive everybody wants.

111
00:04:47,538 --> 00:04:48,789
What else could she have done?

112
00:04:50,624 --> 00:04:52,626
[suspenseful music playing]

113
00:04:57,589 --> 00:05:01,134
You know, a lot of these cars have
the same kind of parking pass Anna did.

114
00:05:01,135 --> 00:05:02,427
Probably for parking over there.

115
00:05:02,428 --> 00:05:04,345
And if they have the same pass as Anna,

116
00:05:04,346 --> 00:05:06,807
they probably have
the same work badge she did, too.

117
00:05:07,891 --> 00:05:09,643
You just figured out our way in.

118
00:05:10,728 --> 00:05:12,895
Hey, hey, we try
to lift someone's work badge,

119
00:05:12,896 --> 00:05:14,147
we could get arrested.

120
00:05:14,148 --> 00:05:15,231
People are looking for us.

121
00:05:15,232 --> 00:05:16,775
We can't allow ourselves
to get put in the system.

122
00:05:18,318 --> 00:05:21,070
We got to get in there.
We can't use Anna's badge.

123
00:05:21,071 --> 00:05:22,780
A dead woman
comes up at the security gate,

124
00:05:22,781 --> 00:05:24,158
we definitely get caught.

125
00:05:26,243 --> 00:05:27,744
[car door closes]

126
00:05:27,745 --> 00:05:29,246
[car alarm chirps]

127
00:05:33,876 --> 00:05:35,878
- [door opens]
- [electric entry bell chimes]

128
00:05:46,138 --> 00:05:47,638
- [exclaims]
- Oh...

129
00:05:47,639 --> 00:05:50,850
- My God! Watch where you're going.
- I am so sorry.

130
00:05:50,851 --> 00:05:53,102
Oh, here, here, here, here. Here.

131
00:05:53,103 --> 00:05:54,353
- Oh, God.
- I am so,

132
00:05:54,354 --> 00:05:55,897
- so sorry.
- Geez.

133
00:05:55,898 --> 00:05:57,441
- Oh, my God.
- Oh...

134
00:06:01,487 --> 00:06:03,780
Here's your stuff. I am so sorry.

135
00:06:03,781 --> 00:06:05,365
[Jacqueline] It's fine. I've got it.

136
00:06:06,867 --> 00:06:08,451
Sorry I snapped. It's just...

137
00:06:08,452 --> 00:06:09,535
a new shirt.

138
00:06:09,536 --> 00:06:10,828
Here, you know what?

139
00:06:10,829 --> 00:06:12,080
Let me give you this
for the dry cleaning.

140
00:06:12,081 --> 00:06:14,248
- I insist.
- It's fine.

141
00:06:14,249 --> 00:06:15,876
Geez.

142
00:06:21,173 --> 00:06:22,840
- [♪ Jamon Scott sings "Remember Me"]
- <i>♪ I never dreamed...</i>

143
00:06:22,841 --> 00:06:24,384
- Two Natty Bohs.
- Thank you.

144
00:06:26,428 --> 00:06:28,221
<i>♪ Livin' in a world...</i>

145
00:06:28,222 --> 00:06:29,598
To Nolan Cahill.

146
00:06:30,599 --> 00:06:32,183
[whispers] What the fuck?

147
00:06:32,184 --> 00:06:36,145
<i>♪ did it come to this...</i>

148
00:06:36,146 --> 00:06:39,774
[regular volume] I met him
first day of Delta training.

149
00:06:39,775 --> 00:06:41,652
Terrified every one of us.

150
00:06:43,278 --> 00:06:45,154
Meanest son of a bitch I ever met.

151
00:06:45,155 --> 00:06:46,697
Is that why you made him
your chief of staff?

152
00:06:46,698 --> 00:06:47,698
A chief of staff

153
00:06:47,699 --> 00:06:49,826
that no one could intimidate?

154
00:06:49,827 --> 00:06:51,953
You can't put a price tag on that.

155
00:06:51,954 --> 00:06:53,913
And I knew that he'd never have a problem

156
00:06:53,914 --> 00:06:56,082
working for someone
he previously commanded

157
00:06:56,083 --> 00:06:57,625
'cause he had no ego.

158
00:06:57,626 --> 00:06:59,920
It was always about the mission.

159
00:07:01,004 --> 00:07:03,506
Molded me in his image.

160
00:07:03,507 --> 00:07:04,674
All of us.

161
00:07:04,675 --> 00:07:05,925
If that's true,

162
00:07:05,926 --> 00:07:08,177
what would make you jump off a roof?

163
00:07:08,178 --> 00:07:09,387
Excuse me?

164
00:07:09,388 --> 00:07:12,056
You said you were molded in his image.

165
00:07:12,057 --> 00:07:13,599
That means you're of
the same character and morality,

166
00:07:13,600 --> 00:07:16,853
more or less. So, given
the same information, the same situation,

167
00:07:16,854 --> 00:07:18,646
same circumstances,

168
00:07:18,647 --> 00:07:19,856
you might react the same way.

169
00:07:19,857 --> 00:07:21,816
Obviously, he knew something you didn't,

170
00:07:21,817 --> 00:07:23,067
so I'm just wondering

171
00:07:23,068 --> 00:07:25,194
what would make you jump off
a building to your death?

172
00:07:25,195 --> 00:07:27,238
I have no goddamn idea.

173
00:07:27,239 --> 00:07:29,448
It's not what your source told you.

174
00:07:29,449 --> 00:07:32,034
We never tortured or killed civilians.

175
00:07:32,035 --> 00:07:34,120
But people were tortured and killed.

176
00:07:34,121 --> 00:07:35,705
After we left.

177
00:07:35,706 --> 00:07:38,125
What exactly did you do in Indonesia?

178
00:07:39,251 --> 00:07:41,794
Look, I know you didn't know Anna Merrick.

179
00:07:41,795 --> 00:07:44,755
Maybe a dead woman's a sunk cost to you,

180
00:07:44,756 --> 00:07:47,592
but her son's still missing. 20 years old,

181
00:07:47,593 --> 00:07:49,678
and very likely with sociopathic killers.

182
00:07:51,054 --> 00:07:52,681
So the truth can only help right now.

183
00:07:54,433 --> 00:07:56,685
Miss? Scotch.

184
00:07:58,353 --> 00:08:00,188
Conversations that require scotch--

185
00:08:00,189 --> 00:08:02,733
Are conversations
that maybe shouldn't be had.

186
00:08:03,525 --> 00:08:05,527
But if there's some way
that you think I can help...

187
00:08:06,778 --> 00:08:07,862
...I'll tell you what I know.

188
00:08:07,863 --> 00:08:09,906
Even if it's things you can't legally say?

189
00:08:09,907 --> 00:08:11,407
Look, I'll dance on that line

190
00:08:11,408 --> 00:08:14,244
best I can without violating my oath.

191
00:08:15,329 --> 00:08:16,662
[waitress] Dewar's okay?

192
00:08:16,663 --> 00:08:17,997
Just fine, thank you.

193
00:08:17,998 --> 00:08:19,749
<i>♪ People lined up ♪</i>

194
00:08:19,750 --> 00:08:22,752
<i>♪ all the way out in the street ♪</i>

195
00:08:22,753 --> 00:08:24,545
<i>♪ waiting for a chance ♪</i>

196
00:08:24,546 --> 00:08:27,256
<i>♪ just to say goodbye to you ♪</i>

197
00:08:27,257 --> 00:08:30,885
<i>♪ telling me what you meant to them ♪</i>

198
00:08:30,886 --> 00:08:32,970
<i>♪ what I meant to you...</i>

199
00:08:32,971 --> 00:08:36,349
Our squadron was dispatched to Indonesia
with a team of scientists.

200
00:08:36,350 --> 00:08:39,060
Something about using rainforest plants

201
00:08:39,061 --> 00:08:41,437
to help find a cure for new

202
00:08:41,438 --> 00:08:44,607
antibiotic-resistant
strains of malaria or...

203
00:08:44,608 --> 00:08:46,567
I-I... Who can remember?

204
00:08:46,568 --> 00:08:50,029
Uncle Sam says you're going
to Asia with a bunch scientists,

205
00:08:50,030 --> 00:08:51,530
you go to Asia.

206
00:08:51,531 --> 00:08:53,824
[uneasy music playing]

207
00:08:53,825 --> 00:08:56,035
Our job was to keep the scientists safe

208
00:08:56,036 --> 00:08:59,664
and to secure the cooperation of
the local government while we were there.

209
00:08:59,665 --> 00:09:03,042
Cahill oversaw
that branch of the operation.

210
00:09:03,043 --> 00:09:06,128
Now, in exchange
for Indonesia's hospitality,

211
00:09:06,129 --> 00:09:09,091
the U.S. agreed
to train their military's special forces.

212
00:09:10,384 --> 00:09:12,469
Which were led by General Reza Putra.

213
00:09:13,053 --> 00:09:15,596
I oversaw that branch of the operation.

214
00:09:15,597 --> 00:09:18,724
Look, I taught his men everything from

215
00:09:18,725 --> 00:09:20,643
weapons training to guerrilla tactics.

216
00:09:20,644 --> 00:09:23,021
When do you get to the part about
slaughtering political dissidents?

217
00:09:26,400 --> 00:09:28,235
Once we got back stateside...

218
00:09:30,153 --> 00:09:31,405
...Putra went after people.

219
00:09:32,030 --> 00:09:35,324
Anyone who was opposed
to his increasing power.

220
00:09:35,325 --> 00:09:38,995
And in the process,
he wiped out an entire village.

221
00:09:38,996 --> 00:09:39,996
Civilians,

222
00:09:39,997 --> 00:09:43,499
kids, elderly.

223
00:09:43,500 --> 00:09:46,502
Innocent, good people.
He was indiscriminate.

224
00:09:46,503 --> 00:09:50,339
Suddenly, he wasn't just some general
that we ran drills with in the jungle.

225
00:09:50,340 --> 00:09:52,383
He was a war criminal.

226
00:09:52,384 --> 00:09:53,593
He was committing...

227
00:09:54,636 --> 00:09:56,512
...just unspeakable acts.

228
00:09:56,513 --> 00:10:00,057
Saddam Hussein, Noriega.

229
00:10:00,058 --> 00:10:03,394
We were even friends with bin Laden
when he was fighting the Russians.

230
00:10:03,395 --> 00:10:05,479
Wouldn't be the first time
America's been burned.

231
00:10:05,480 --> 00:10:08,984
Still, if my connection to Putra
were to be made public...

232
00:10:10,444 --> 00:10:12,528
...it would derail everything
that my team and I

233
00:10:12,529 --> 00:10:14,197
have worked for for the past few years.

234
00:10:15,407 --> 00:10:18,034
All right? This source of yours

235
00:10:18,035 --> 00:10:21,245
is after stolen records
connecting me to a monster.

236
00:10:21,246 --> 00:10:22,955
She thinks you're the monster.

237
00:10:22,956 --> 00:10:25,416
Well, then you tell her that
she's pissing up the wrong damn tree,

238
00:10:25,417 --> 00:10:27,460
because I had nothing
to do with what Putra

239
00:10:27,461 --> 00:10:30,046
did to those people. In fact,

240
00:10:30,047 --> 00:10:33,466
the U.S. government sent a team down there

241
00:10:33,467 --> 00:10:36,260
to deal with Putra
the second they caught wind of it.

242
00:10:36,261 --> 00:10:38,179
Well, something made Cahill

243
00:10:38,180 --> 00:10:40,473
follow Anna Merrick onto that subway,

244
00:10:40,474 --> 00:10:42,100
and something made him jump off that roof.

245
00:10:43,310 --> 00:10:45,394
I'm gonna get that drive
and find out what that is.

246
00:10:45,395 --> 00:10:47,104
Well, I goddamn hope you do.

247
00:10:47,105 --> 00:10:49,900
And when you find it,
you do the right thing...

248
00:10:52,903 --> 00:10:54,363
...and return it to me.

249
00:10:56,490 --> 00:10:57,948
You seem to want this thing pretty bad

250
00:10:57,949 --> 00:10:59,742
for someone who doesn't know what's on it.

251
00:10:59,743 --> 00:11:01,452
Well, I could say the same of you.

252
00:11:01,453 --> 00:11:03,454
You want it bad enough
to send a team of mercenaries

253
00:11:03,455 --> 00:11:04,872
in tactical gear after me.

254
00:11:04,873 --> 00:11:07,124
I don't act outside the law.

255
00:11:07,125 --> 00:11:09,877
The CIA does. Maybe you leaned on them.

256
00:11:09,878 --> 00:11:12,963
I already told you I have
no involvement with that agency.

257
00:11:12,964 --> 00:11:15,091
It'll be a cold day in hell
that they take orders

258
00:11:15,092 --> 00:11:16,676
from a U.S. congressman.

259
00:11:18,220 --> 00:11:20,679
Look, all I did was redirect you

260
00:11:20,680 --> 00:11:23,057
to the Department of Defense
when you started poking around.

261
00:11:23,058 --> 00:11:24,893
Is that who
Snap, Crackle and Pop work for?

262
00:11:25,811 --> 00:11:27,019
Is that what you call them?

263
00:11:27,020 --> 00:11:28,688
They wouldn't tell me their names.

264
00:11:31,483 --> 00:11:32,859
Look, bottom line, Reacher...

265
00:11:33,985 --> 00:11:36,654
...regardless of my personal interests,

266
00:11:36,655 --> 00:11:38,531
whatever's on that drive

267
00:11:38,532 --> 00:11:41,033
is the property of the United States Army,

268
00:11:41,034 --> 00:11:44,745
and I just want to see it get returned
to the Pentagon where it belongs.

269
00:11:44,746 --> 00:11:46,373
I'll find that drive.

270
00:11:47,582 --> 00:11:49,126
And once I see what's on it...

271
00:11:50,419 --> 00:11:52,378
...I'll decide what to do with it.

272
00:11:52,379 --> 00:11:54,381
[thunder rumbling]

273
00:12:14,651 --> 00:12:15,818
He's probably gonna be a while.

274
00:12:15,819 --> 00:12:16,820
[Elspeth] That's okay.

275
00:12:18,530 --> 00:12:20,739
John Sampson will not drink from the well

276
00:12:20,740 --> 00:12:22,658
when mourning his best friend.

277
00:12:22,659 --> 00:12:24,494
All the same to a dead man.

278
00:12:28,290 --> 00:12:29,875
Do you know what a Jonah is?

279
00:12:31,084 --> 00:12:33,669
Among sailors,
a Jonah is the person on the ship

280
00:12:33,670 --> 00:12:36,547
that brings nothing but misfortune.

281
00:12:36,548 --> 00:12:38,757
Every time he comes on board,

282
00:12:38,758 --> 00:12:40,510
bad luck follows.

283
00:12:42,596 --> 00:12:44,139
You're a Jonah, Mr. Reacher.

284
00:12:45,223 --> 00:12:47,558
Like my mother said,

285
00:12:47,559 --> 00:12:49,310
trouble always seems to find me.

286
00:12:49,311 --> 00:12:51,270
I hope you got what you needed from John,

287
00:12:51,271 --> 00:12:53,398
'cause I'd prefer it
if you stay away from us now.

288
00:13:00,864 --> 00:13:02,781
Can I get a ride back to the hotel?

289
00:13:02,782 --> 00:13:04,284
Fuck off.

290
00:13:10,248 --> 00:13:12,958
[tense music playing]

291
00:13:12,959 --> 00:13:14,961
[indistinct radio chatter]

292
00:13:17,172 --> 00:13:18,881
We don't get a lot of walk-ons.

293
00:13:18,882 --> 00:13:21,635
Yeah, car's in the shop.
Had the Lyft drop me at the bagel place.

294
00:13:27,015 --> 00:13:28,891
Why is she looking at the ground?

295
00:13:28,892 --> 00:13:31,143
She doesn't want cameras
to get a good look at her face.

296
00:13:31,144 --> 00:13:32,812
This is taking too long.

297
00:13:36,107 --> 00:13:37,733
She's definitely gonna get busted.

298
00:13:37,734 --> 00:13:39,109
No, she's not.

299
00:13:39,110 --> 00:13:40,069
[off-key beep]

300
00:13:40,070 --> 00:13:41,737
Seems to be a problem.

301
00:13:41,738 --> 00:13:42,863
You got to put the chip up.

302
00:13:42,864 --> 00:13:46,284
Ah. See, this is what you get
when they've only got decaf left.

303
00:13:49,204 --> 00:13:50,622
Have a good one.

304
00:13:54,960 --> 00:13:56,043
Yes.

305
00:13:56,044 --> 00:13:58,921
[Cheryl] You can't just email distribution
and then hope that they'll follow through,

306
00:13:58,922 --> 00:14:01,298
'cause they won't.
That's why I have to be copied

307
00:14:01,299 --> 00:14:02,716
on everything moving forward.

308
00:14:02,717 --> 00:14:04,386
Okay? Okay.

309
00:14:06,638 --> 00:14:10,182
Hi. Can you help me? Shannon Thomas.

310
00:14:10,183 --> 00:14:12,768
I'm totally lost.
I was transferred here from the Pentagon.

311
00:14:12,769 --> 00:14:15,229
I'm supposed
to be taking over for Anna Merrick.

312
00:14:15,230 --> 00:14:17,856
Cheryl Brewster. So awful about Anna.

313
00:14:17,857 --> 00:14:19,149
Did you know her?

314
00:14:19,150 --> 00:14:20,568
Didn't have the pleasure.

315
00:14:20,569 --> 00:14:21,652
Come on.

316
00:14:21,653 --> 00:14:22,779
I'll get you set up.

317
00:14:23,822 --> 00:14:25,656
Anna was so sweet.

318
00:14:25,657 --> 00:14:28,200
But I guess you never really know
what someone's going through, do you?

319
00:14:28,201 --> 00:14:29,202
No.

320
00:14:29,995 --> 00:14:33,290
Embarrassed to admit I really don't know
what I'm supposed to be doing here.

321
00:14:34,457 --> 00:14:36,584
I got reassigned last minute.

322
00:14:36,585 --> 00:14:39,211
It's pretty simple. We're converting
physical files to digital.

323
00:14:39,212 --> 00:14:40,754
Less space, less waste.

324
00:14:40,755 --> 00:14:43,675
You'll be scanning documents and uploading
them in our digital storage system.

325
00:14:44,926 --> 00:14:47,887
It's busywork, but busywork
that pays time and a half.

326
00:14:50,807 --> 00:14:53,851
Ah, yeah. This was Anna's workstation.

327
00:14:53,852 --> 00:14:56,604
I know it's a little morbid,
but the productivity logs

328
00:14:56,605 --> 00:14:58,814
are tied to the workstation number...
Blah, blah, blah.

329
00:14:58,815 --> 00:15:01,442
I'll make sure someone comes by
to clean out her desk.

330
00:15:01,443 --> 00:15:02,861
That's okay. It's fine.

331
00:15:04,237 --> 00:15:06,113
Logging you in under Anna's ID

332
00:15:06,114 --> 00:15:08,408
so you can pick up where she left off.

333
00:15:10,827 --> 00:15:12,787
I'll switch it
into your name by the end of day.

334
00:15:14,039 --> 00:15:15,789
All the files are numbered.

335
00:15:15,790 --> 00:15:18,667
That's what you'll name the file
after it's scanned.

336
00:15:18,668 --> 00:15:20,127
When you've scanned
all the files in the box,

337
00:15:20,128 --> 00:15:21,545
drop the box and its contents

338
00:15:21,546 --> 00:15:24,340
in one of the carts in the hallway
marked for incineration.

339
00:15:24,341 --> 00:15:25,841
Then you start a new box.

340
00:15:25,842 --> 00:15:27,009
Any questions?

341
00:15:27,010 --> 00:15:28,136
Nope.

342
00:15:30,013 --> 00:15:31,306
I think I got it.

343
00:15:36,144 --> 00:15:38,687
Wait, isn't that the woman
you stole the badge from?

344
00:15:38,688 --> 00:15:40,022
Fuck.

345
00:15:40,023 --> 00:15:41,399
How fast does she eat?

346
00:15:52,243 --> 00:15:54,536
I'm sorry, I must have
forgotten my badge today.

347
00:15:54,537 --> 00:15:55,871
Name?

348
00:15:55,872 --> 00:15:57,415
Jacqueline Reed.

349
00:16:08,885 --> 00:16:11,887
Ma'am, your badge
was swiped in ten minutes ago.

350
00:16:11,888 --> 00:16:13,430
- [phone rings]
- What?

351
00:16:13,431 --> 00:16:14,473
[Jacob] <i>You got to get out of there.</i>

352
00:16:14,474 --> 00:16:15,684
They know you stole the badge.

353
00:16:16,935 --> 00:16:18,477
[indistinct chatter]

354
00:16:18,478 --> 00:16:19,937
Shit.

355
00:16:19,938 --> 00:16:22,106
[security guard] Excuse me, ma'am,
can I talk to you, please?

356
00:16:22,107 --> 00:16:23,983
Ma'am, I need you to wait.

357
00:16:24,984 --> 00:16:27,069
Yeah, I got her. She's right here.

358
00:16:27,070 --> 00:16:28,278
Ma'am!

359
00:16:28,279 --> 00:16:30,114
I need backup. We're on the second floor.

360
00:16:30,115 --> 00:16:31,573
Whoa. What's going on?

361
00:16:31,574 --> 00:16:33,410
[exciting music playing]

362
00:16:34,160 --> 00:16:35,078
Oh!

363
00:16:39,874 --> 00:16:41,626
[clattering]

364
00:16:47,507 --> 00:16:49,968
[alarm blaring]

365
00:16:56,266 --> 00:16:58,685
Uh, is it good that she's running?

366
00:17:04,607 --> 00:17:06,316
[gate guard] You! Hold it!

367
00:17:06,317 --> 00:17:07,317
- Stop!
- [Russell] Come on, hurry!

368
00:17:07,318 --> 00:17:08,485
- [gate guard] Get back here now!
- Go!

369
00:17:08,486 --> 00:17:10,196
- Go, go!
- Get out of the car! Damn it!

370
00:17:25,503 --> 00:17:26,796
[car engine revving]

371
00:17:28,381 --> 00:17:30,383
[dramatic music playing]

372
00:17:35,221 --> 00:17:37,807
Let's do it the easy way
this time, Reacher.

373
00:17:41,853 --> 00:17:43,687
No.

374
00:17:43,688 --> 00:17:45,315
[exciting music playing]

375
00:17:46,316 --> 00:17:47,484
[Foster] Keep him alive!

376
00:18:11,674 --> 00:18:13,551
Break formation. Tactical dispersal.

377
00:18:28,233 --> 00:18:29,776
[clattering]

378
00:18:33,696 --> 00:18:35,114
[grunting]

379
00:18:58,805 --> 00:18:59,888
[shouts]

380
00:18:59,889 --> 00:19:02,308
[grunting]

381
00:19:13,820 --> 00:19:15,446
[clattering in distance]

382
00:19:25,164 --> 00:19:26,583
[grunting]

383
00:19:46,603 --> 00:19:48,187
[shouts]

384
00:20:17,300 --> 00:20:19,302
[grunting]

385
00:20:27,310 --> 00:20:28,352
[groans]

386
00:20:28,353 --> 00:20:29,895
[shouts]

387
00:20:29,896 --> 00:20:30,855
What was your plan?

388
00:20:33,066 --> 00:20:34,399
Uh...

389
00:20:34,400 --> 00:20:36,443
maim you, shove you in a van,

390
00:20:36,444 --> 00:20:37,986
ask you some questions.

391
00:20:37,987 --> 00:20:40,447
[sirens wailing in distance]

392
00:20:40,448 --> 00:20:41,448
[exclaims]

393
00:20:41,449 --> 00:20:43,451
[groaning]

394
00:20:49,374 --> 00:20:52,084
No. No, no, no, no, no, no, no, no!

395
00:20:52,085 --> 00:20:53,502
Fuck. Fuck! Aah!

396
00:20:53,503 --> 00:20:56,422
- God! [panting]
- [grunts]

397
00:21:00,551 --> 00:21:01,927
Prick.

398
00:21:01,928 --> 00:21:04,430
You're lucky I didn't throw you in, too.

399
00:21:29,247 --> 00:21:30,330
[Tamara] It was just the one file.

400
00:21:30,331 --> 00:21:32,624
Anna scanned it, deleted it
and sent it to the incinerator.

401
00:21:32,625 --> 00:21:33,625
Never uploaded it

402
00:21:33,626 --> 00:21:35,043
- to the government server.
- [Reacher] <i>She must have</i>

403
00:21:35,044 --> 00:21:37,254
downloaded it to a jump drive
before she deleted it.

404
00:21:37,255 --> 00:21:38,588
Yeah, that's what we're thinking, too.

405
00:21:38,589 --> 00:21:39,756
<i>So whatever's on that drive</i>

406
00:21:39,757 --> 00:21:41,216
is the only copy in existence

407
00:21:41,217 --> 00:21:43,343
<i>- by design.</i>
- My sister's design?

408
00:21:43,344 --> 00:21:45,387
<i>More likely whoever
she was getting that data for.</i>

409
00:21:45,388 --> 00:21:48,223
If it contains the only copy in the world
of whatever documents are on it,

410
00:21:48,224 --> 00:21:49,641
that drive becomes a lot more valuable.

411
00:21:49,642 --> 00:21:52,519
<i>Feels like whoever wanted it
is probably looking to sell it.</i>

412
00:21:52,520 --> 00:21:53,854
Or they don't want us
to see what they took

413
00:21:53,855 --> 00:21:55,188
so we can't plan a defense against it.

414
00:21:55,189 --> 00:21:56,565
<i>Won't know till we know.</i>

415
00:21:56,566 --> 00:21:58,275
Let's hope we don't find out too late.

416
00:21:58,276 --> 00:21:59,484
<i>Okay, I'll see you in Philly.</i>

417
00:21:59,485 --> 00:22:02,362
We're almost back.
Just in traffic south of the city.

418
00:22:02,363 --> 00:22:03,572
What is the point of nighttime

419
00:22:03,573 --> 00:22:05,782
<i>construction if they keep
this equipment in the road all day?</i>

420
00:22:05,783 --> 00:22:07,325
[intriguing music playing]

421
00:22:07,326 --> 00:22:08,785
How long have you been stuck in traffic?

422
00:22:08,786 --> 00:22:09,870
[Tamara] <i>Couple exits.</i>

423
00:22:09,871 --> 00:22:11,997
But it gets really bad in this bottleneck.

424
00:22:11,998 --> 00:22:13,457
Can you see the next off-ramp?

425
00:22:13,458 --> 00:22:15,876
[Jacob] <i>Uh, exit 20.</i>

426
00:22:15,877 --> 00:22:18,045
Columbus Boulevard and Washington.

427
00:22:18,046 --> 00:22:20,922
Plum, look up the nearest
subway station to exit 20.

428
00:22:20,923 --> 00:22:22,049
[Russell] <i>Yeah, copy that.</i>

429
00:22:22,050 --> 00:22:23,133
What are you thinking?

430
00:22:23,134 --> 00:22:24,885
Thinking I might know
why Anna got out of her car

431
00:22:24,886 --> 00:22:25,969
and onto the subway.

432
00:22:25,970 --> 00:22:29,806
- Tasker-Morris station.
- That's where Anna bought her SEPTA pass.

433
00:22:29,807 --> 00:22:33,060
She was probably stuck in the same traffic
when she was bringing Lila

434
00:22:33,061 --> 00:22:34,478
<i>- the drive.</i>
- So she took the first exit

435
00:22:34,479 --> 00:22:36,188
after traffic got bad,

436
00:22:36,189 --> 00:22:38,690
went to the nearest SEPTA station
and got on the next subway.

437
00:22:38,691 --> 00:22:40,192
<i>Anna had a clock to beat.</i>

438
00:22:40,193 --> 00:22:42,444
She was given a deadline. Can you get off

439
00:22:42,445 --> 00:22:43,570
<i>that road and get to that subway station?</i>

440
00:22:43,571 --> 00:22:44,988
[Jacob] Yeah, we're almost
through the bottleneck.

441
00:22:44,989 --> 00:22:45,989
<i>I'll meet you</i>

442
00:22:45,990 --> 00:22:47,408
at Tasker-Morris as soon as I can.

443
00:22:59,212 --> 00:23:01,214
[mournful music playing]

444
00:23:10,640 --> 00:23:11,641
You good?

445
00:23:17,063 --> 00:23:19,439
There's no way that Ben's okay, is there?

446
00:23:19,440 --> 00:23:21,316
We don't know that yet.

447
00:23:21,317 --> 00:23:22,819
Do you think he's alive?

448
00:23:23,903 --> 00:23:25,987
Come on. You're a city cop.

449
00:23:25,988 --> 00:23:28,950
You've worked hard cases before.
You have instincts.

450
00:23:30,701 --> 00:23:32,328
Is my nephew alive?

451
00:23:33,746 --> 00:23:35,832
I've seen no evidence
that confirms Ben is dead.

452
00:23:38,918 --> 00:23:42,212
I feel like I have no control

453
00:23:42,213 --> 00:23:44,256
over anything that's happening,

454
00:23:44,257 --> 00:23:46,134
and I have no power to stop it.

455
00:23:47,885 --> 00:23:49,428
I'm spinning out, you know?

456
00:23:50,680 --> 00:23:53,515
Hey. Hey. Listen to me.

457
00:23:53,516 --> 00:23:55,226
I've been a cop 15 years.

458
00:23:56,394 --> 00:23:58,646
I've seen terrible shit
happen to good people.

459
00:24:00,857 --> 00:24:03,525
It's easy to get lost in the aftermath.

460
00:24:03,526 --> 00:24:06,027
We don't have the luxury right now.

461
00:24:06,028 --> 00:24:08,196
We got killers and feds
raining down on us,

462
00:24:08,197 --> 00:24:10,448
and a case that
doesn't make any fucking sense.

463
00:24:10,449 --> 00:24:11,867
We need you,

464
00:24:11,868 --> 00:24:15,120
with your feet planted firm
and your head in the game.

465
00:24:15,121 --> 00:24:17,414
So when that darkness
starts to wrap around you

466
00:24:17,415 --> 00:24:19,749
and you feel like
you're about to spin out,

467
00:24:19,750 --> 00:24:22,170
know there's people who care for you.

468
00:24:23,546 --> 00:24:25,047
Myself included.

469
00:24:27,758 --> 00:24:29,259
Oh, um...

470
00:24:29,260 --> 00:24:30,928
you reminded me.

471
00:24:32,889 --> 00:24:34,431
Took this from Anna's workstation.

472
00:24:34,432 --> 00:24:36,267
I thought you should have it.

473
00:24:41,522 --> 00:24:42,523
Thank you.

474
00:24:43,399 --> 00:24:46,319
Figured nobody would have a problem
with turkey and cheese.

475
00:24:49,322 --> 00:24:50,698
Everything okay?

476
00:24:51,782 --> 00:24:52,783
We're good.

477
00:24:55,369 --> 00:24:56,537
He's here.

478
00:25:00,583 --> 00:25:02,042
Oh, shit.

479
00:25:02,043 --> 00:25:03,336
I didn't get him a hoagie.

480
00:25:05,796 --> 00:25:07,673
Oh. Sorry, man.

481
00:25:08,382 --> 00:25:10,759
All right, so, this is where Anna came.

482
00:25:10,760 --> 00:25:12,552
She was desperate.

483
00:25:12,553 --> 00:25:14,721
You said she was at zero hour,
so break it down.

484
00:25:14,722 --> 00:25:16,473
Let's assume we're right.

485
00:25:16,474 --> 00:25:19,017
The OnlyFans woman
was murdered and her killers--

486
00:25:19,018 --> 00:25:21,478
probably the Big Red Machine--
kidnapped Ben.

487
00:25:21,479 --> 00:25:24,356
Leverage to get Anna to bring them
the drive instead of giving it to Lila.

488
00:25:24,357 --> 00:25:27,234
"Get us the drive by a certain time
or we kill your son."

489
00:25:27,235 --> 00:25:28,818
That's why she couldn't
sit in traffic anymore.

490
00:25:28,819 --> 00:25:31,571
So she took the subway
to try to save Ben,

491
00:25:31,572 --> 00:25:34,242
but that doesn't make sense, because...

492
00:25:35,326 --> 00:25:36,410
Because what?

493
00:25:37,078 --> 00:25:38,912
Uh, I don't know, man. Nothing.

494
00:25:38,913 --> 00:25:40,665
I think what Plum is trying to say is...

495
00:25:42,208 --> 00:25:44,000
...we know your sister
didn't have the drive on the train,

496
00:25:44,001 --> 00:25:46,127
and she couldn't save Ben
without the drive,

497
00:25:46,128 --> 00:25:48,088
so by the time Anna got on the subway,

498
00:25:48,089 --> 00:25:49,798
she already knew her son was dead.

499
00:25:49,799 --> 00:25:52,425
No, no, no, no, no, no.
That-that doesn't play out.

500
00:25:52,426 --> 00:25:54,135
Why would they kill their bargaining chip?

501
00:25:54,136 --> 00:25:56,346
Kidnapping's rarely a clean crime.

502
00:25:56,347 --> 00:25:59,975
It's hard enough to control a child,
let alone a 250-pound linebacker.

503
00:25:59,976 --> 00:26:02,644
So things broke bad and Ben wound up dead.

504
00:26:02,645 --> 00:26:04,021
I don't want to be right,
but I think I am.

505
00:26:04,605 --> 00:26:06,147
How could she know Ben was dead?

506
00:26:06,148 --> 00:26:08,775
She was probably
getting proof of life updates.

507
00:26:08,776 --> 00:26:10,278
Phone calls, maybe a video.

508
00:26:11,279 --> 00:26:13,446
Then they refused to do it anymore.

509
00:26:13,447 --> 00:26:16,992
Anna was stuck in traffic, missing their
deadline would be bad news for Ben,

510
00:26:16,993 --> 00:26:19,745
so she called,
insisted on seeing he was still okay.

511
00:26:21,163 --> 00:26:22,414
They wouldn't put him on the phone.

512
00:26:22,415 --> 00:26:24,791
'Cause they couldn't put him on the phone.

513
00:26:24,792 --> 00:26:26,626
That's how she knew he was already dead.

514
00:26:26,627 --> 00:26:29,129
[Russell] So Anna couldn't save Ben,

515
00:26:29,130 --> 00:26:31,172
why even get on the subway?

516
00:26:31,173 --> 00:26:33,843
She couldn't stand to just sit there
after what they did.

517
00:26:35,469 --> 00:26:38,680
She needed to avenge him.
She got to the off-ramp...

518
00:26:38,681 --> 00:26:39,973
and she took it.

519
00:26:39,974 --> 00:26:41,433
He's right.

520
00:26:41,434 --> 00:26:42,809
When I saw Anna, she was a grieving,

521
00:26:42,810 --> 00:26:44,728
distraught mother with a gun.

522
00:26:44,729 --> 00:26:46,313
She wanted her pound of flesh.

523
00:26:46,314 --> 00:26:49,107
From professional mercenaries?
She was a government clerk.

524
00:26:49,108 --> 00:26:51,818
She didn't care. She probably grabbed
the black coat she had in the car

525
00:26:51,819 --> 00:26:53,862
to cover the white shirt she had on,
hoping it would help her

526
00:26:53,863 --> 00:26:56,031
sneak up on Ben's killers.

527
00:26:56,032 --> 00:26:57,824
She probably
brought the gun to protect herself

528
00:26:57,825 --> 00:26:59,868
and Ben after she gave
the drive to the kidnappers

529
00:26:59,869 --> 00:27:01,119
so that she could get out of there,

530
00:27:01,120 --> 00:27:02,872
but now she was just
using it to get payback.

531
00:27:03,831 --> 00:27:06,291
But if she had a plan...

532
00:27:06,292 --> 00:27:07,877
why would she kill herself?

533
00:27:13,174 --> 00:27:14,257
What?

534
00:27:14,258 --> 00:27:16,468
[foreboding music playing]

535
00:27:16,469 --> 00:27:18,054
When I approached Anna...

536
00:27:19,555 --> 00:27:20,723
...I told her I was a cop.

537
00:27:21,766 --> 00:27:23,058
She was smart.

538
00:27:23,059 --> 00:27:25,853
She knew I'd have to arrest her,
and any hope for revenge would be over.

539
00:27:26,437 --> 00:27:28,105
She already felt like
she failed Ben in life.

540
00:27:28,689 --> 00:27:30,231
Now she was failing him in death.

541
00:27:30,232 --> 00:27:32,151
The guilt and pain was too much for her.

542
00:27:33,361 --> 00:27:36,113
You told her that you were a cop?

543
00:27:36,739 --> 00:27:38,198
I wanted her to-to feel like

544
00:27:38,199 --> 00:27:40,408
she was with somebody in control
when she was so clearly

545
00:27:40,409 --> 00:27:42,035
out of control.

546
00:27:42,036 --> 00:27:43,579
I thought it would
de-escalate the situation.

547
00:27:44,413 --> 00:27:45,664
I wanted to help her.

548
00:27:47,541 --> 00:27:49,126
I didn't.

549
00:27:49,960 --> 00:27:52,170
The jump drive-- Anna had it
when she left for Philly.

550
00:27:52,171 --> 00:27:55,298
No way she was gonna let Ben's killers
get their hands on that drive.

551
00:27:55,299 --> 00:27:56,217
So she got rid of it.

552
00:27:57,134 --> 00:27:58,386
I think I know where she put it.

553
00:27:59,261 --> 00:28:00,970
The mail that we found in Anna's car.

554
00:28:00,971 --> 00:28:04,140
The only open letter
was that credit card offer.

555
00:28:04,141 --> 00:28:06,393
I don't remember seeing a return envelope.

556
00:28:06,394 --> 00:28:09,604
You know, the kind that has a "No Postage
Necessary" thing in the corner?

557
00:28:09,605 --> 00:28:10,772
She mailed the drive,

558
00:28:10,773 --> 00:28:12,857
and maybe her phone,
before getting on the subway.

559
00:28:12,858 --> 00:28:13,775
To who?

560
00:28:13,776 --> 00:28:14,692
To Lila.

561
00:28:14,693 --> 00:28:16,152
So if Anna died getting vengeance,

562
00:28:16,153 --> 00:28:18,488
at least Ben's killers
would be exposed by her story.

563
00:28:18,489 --> 00:28:19,989
The fourth estate.

564
00:28:19,990 --> 00:28:21,658
Last line of defense against tyranny.

565
00:28:21,659 --> 00:28:23,618
So the thing we've been
looking for this entire time

566
00:28:23,619 --> 00:28:24,994
has been at Lila's hotel?

567
00:28:24,995 --> 00:28:26,913
It's the last place
Anna knew Lila was staying.

568
00:28:26,914 --> 00:28:28,832
But Lila had to leave the hotel
after getting attacked,

569
00:28:28,833 --> 00:28:30,084
- before the drive was delivered.
- [phone rings]

570
00:28:33,045 --> 00:28:34,212
Docherty.

571
00:28:34,213 --> 00:28:36,214
Wh-- You gave him your burner number?

572
00:28:36,215 --> 00:28:37,216
Hey.

573
00:28:41,429 --> 00:28:42,638
Okay.

574
00:28:46,100 --> 00:28:47,518
Thanks.

575
00:28:51,272 --> 00:28:52,690
They found Ben.

576
00:28:55,651 --> 00:28:56,861
I'm so sorry.

577
00:29:06,120 --> 00:29:08,204
I don't want him going
to the morgue alone.

578
00:29:08,205 --> 00:29:10,165
- Plum and I will go with him.
- Yeah.

579
00:29:10,166 --> 00:29:13,001
You meet Lila at her old hotel.
Get that fucking drive.

580
00:29:13,002 --> 00:29:14,003
I'll let you know when I have it.

581
00:29:15,254 --> 00:29:16,754
[line ringing]

582
00:29:16,755 --> 00:29:18,674
Lila? It's Reacher.

583
00:29:19,258 --> 00:29:20,758
[Lila] <i>Can you check in the back?</i>

584
00:29:20,759 --> 00:29:22,719
We don't keep anything in the back.
Old mail

585
00:29:22,720 --> 00:29:25,221
and lost and found stays up at the desk.

586
00:29:25,222 --> 00:29:26,639
Can I talk to a manager?

587
00:29:26,640 --> 00:29:28,016
Uh, Gene's in the Poconos till next week.

588
00:29:28,017 --> 00:29:29,517
He'll tell you the same.

589
00:29:29,518 --> 00:29:30,936
Thanks for the help.

590
00:29:33,898 --> 00:29:36,065
Is there any other place
Anna would have sent the drive to you?

591
00:29:36,066 --> 00:29:37,484
It's the only place she knew.

592
00:29:37,485 --> 00:29:38,985
I can't believe it wasn't there.

593
00:29:38,986 --> 00:29:40,403
I got to get to the morgue.

594
00:29:40,404 --> 00:29:42,655
- The morgue?
- Anna's son was killed.

595
00:29:42,656 --> 00:29:45,033
- Body was just found.
- Dear God.

596
00:29:45,034 --> 00:29:46,242
Mother and son.

597
00:29:46,243 --> 00:29:48,077
Was it the same people
who keep coming after us?

598
00:29:48,078 --> 00:29:49,370
Most likely.

599
00:29:49,371 --> 00:29:52,832
Working theory is Ben was a pawn
to make Anna to bring them the drive.

600
00:29:52,833 --> 00:29:55,793
So, I'm responsible for Anna's death
and that of her only child.

601
00:29:55,794 --> 00:29:57,379
[sinister music playing]

602
00:29:58,631 --> 00:30:00,507
- What's wrong?
- We're being followed.

603
00:30:00,508 --> 00:30:01,799
The men who attacked you at this hotel

604
00:30:01,800 --> 00:30:04,553
must have been staking the place out
in case you showed up again.

605
00:30:05,554 --> 00:30:06,721
What do we do?

606
00:30:06,722 --> 00:30:09,557
Someone's got enough money
for an endless supply of assholes.

607
00:30:09,558 --> 00:30:11,017
I need to find out who.

608
00:30:11,018 --> 00:30:12,436
Come on.

609
00:30:16,857 --> 00:30:18,859
[grunting]

610
00:30:29,870 --> 00:30:30,870
- No!
- I'll deal with these guys

611
00:30:30,871 --> 00:30:32,790
and meet you at the hotel.

612
00:30:34,416 --> 00:30:35,918
[gun cocks]

613
00:30:41,674 --> 00:30:42,883
The drive.

614
00:30:43,759 --> 00:30:44,968
Left pocket.

615
00:30:44,969 --> 00:30:46,220
Get it, then.

616
00:30:49,181 --> 00:30:51,432
Unless you want to shoot me here
in front of all these people

617
00:30:51,433 --> 00:30:55,144
and risk getting arrested the same day
you get the information you've been after,

618
00:30:55,145 --> 00:30:56,355
you get it.

619
00:30:59,692 --> 00:31:01,694
[grunting]

620
00:31:04,154 --> 00:31:05,531
[people exclaiming, screaming]

621
00:31:16,125 --> 00:31:17,417
Who do you work for?

622
00:31:17,418 --> 00:31:18,752
- Fuck you.
- [horn blows]

623
00:31:22,423 --> 00:31:24,258
Aah!

624
00:31:25,301 --> 00:31:27,303
[groaning]

625
00:31:28,679 --> 00:31:30,264
Ow! [groaning]

626
00:31:35,394 --> 00:31:37,730
Answer my questions
or I'll take your other hand.

627
00:31:40,524 --> 00:31:41,899
Aah!

628
00:31:41,900 --> 00:31:44,069
[zapping]

629
00:31:48,032 --> 00:31:49,490
Oh, shit.

630
00:31:49,491 --> 00:31:50,951
[man in distance] Down here
on the platform!

631
00:31:52,828 --> 00:31:54,830
[propulsive music playing]

632
00:31:55,914 --> 00:31:57,707
[grunts]

633
00:31:57,708 --> 00:31:58,876
[cop] Hands up!

634
00:32:00,044 --> 00:32:00,961
Show me your hands.

635
00:32:04,006 --> 00:32:05,799
On the move!

636
00:32:08,260 --> 00:32:09,762
[honking]

637
00:32:11,805 --> 00:32:13,682
[grunting, shouting]

638
00:32:20,439 --> 00:32:23,149
We need backup on the Tasker-Morris
southbound platform.

639
00:32:23,150 --> 00:32:24,943
Get guys on the exits now.

640
00:32:29,281 --> 00:32:31,241
[grunting, panting]

641
00:32:35,412 --> 00:32:37,414
[indistinct chatter]

642
00:32:46,965 --> 00:32:48,967
[police radio chatter]

643
00:32:51,679 --> 00:32:53,721
[man] Hey.

644
00:32:53,722 --> 00:32:55,224
A little help?

645
00:33:00,062 --> 00:33:02,648
- [indistinct chatter]
- [female cop] He didn't come this way.

646
00:33:03,482 --> 00:33:05,109
[male cop] I'll head east, you go west.

647
00:33:11,907 --> 00:33:13,324
Thanks.

648
00:33:13,325 --> 00:33:15,201
Hey, you know
how to get to the Liberty Gardens Hotel

649
00:33:15,202 --> 00:33:16,578
through these tunnels?

650
00:33:41,186 --> 00:33:43,020
Where did they find him?

651
00:33:43,021 --> 00:33:46,817
He was found around 9th and Callowhill.

652
00:33:49,987 --> 00:33:51,405
Who called it in?

653
00:33:56,618 --> 00:34:00,330
A garbageman reported
a smell from a dumpster.

654
00:34:02,416 --> 00:34:03,917
I'll give you a moment.

655
00:34:05,961 --> 00:34:07,171
[door opens]

656
00:34:08,380 --> 00:34:09,631
[door closes]

657
00:34:10,716 --> 00:34:12,760
[somber music playing]

658
00:34:14,178 --> 00:34:16,597
All Anna was dealing with...

659
00:34:20,017 --> 00:34:22,311
...she never called me for help.

660
00:34:23,437 --> 00:34:25,147
Because she knew I'm just a...

661
00:34:26,857 --> 00:34:29,150
...glorified...

662
00:34:29,151 --> 00:34:31,028
crossing guard with a badge.

663
00:34:33,489 --> 00:34:37,284
Feckless, useless f-fuckin'...

664
00:34:38,827 --> 00:34:40,537
...meter maid.

665
00:34:47,377 --> 00:34:49,213
I wouldn't have called me, either.

666
00:34:59,515 --> 00:35:01,517
[sniffles softly]

667
00:35:05,938 --> 00:35:07,940
I have nothing now.

668
00:35:09,900 --> 00:35:11,902
I am completely...

669
00:35:13,362 --> 00:35:14,488
...alone.

670
00:35:35,259 --> 00:35:37,261
[train rumbling in distance]

671
00:35:42,057 --> 00:35:43,224
- [device beeps]
- [radio static]

672
00:35:43,225 --> 00:35:44,308
[merc leader] <i>Can you hear me?</i>

673
00:35:44,309 --> 00:35:47,019
<i>Rendezvous at Liberty Gardens ASAP.</i>

674
00:35:47,020 --> 00:35:49,772
<i>Bennett, do you copy? Over.</i>

675
00:35:49,773 --> 00:35:51,607
<i>Tyler?</i>

676
00:35:51,608 --> 00:35:53,192
[menacing music playing]

677
00:35:53,193 --> 00:35:55,403
<i>Rendezvous at Liberty Gardens ASAP.</i>

678
00:35:55,404 --> 00:35:57,489
<i>We're moving in on the Princess' tower.</i>

679
00:36:00,826 --> 00:36:02,952
[sighs]

680
00:36:02,953 --> 00:36:05,288
Come on, Lila.

681
00:36:05,289 --> 00:36:06,957
Shit.

682
00:36:25,726 --> 00:36:27,728
[elevator bell chimes]

683
00:36:35,027 --> 00:36:36,944
[gasps]

684
00:36:36,945 --> 00:36:38,614
[exhales]

685
00:36:40,991 --> 00:36:42,701
[speaking Indonesian] You startled me.

686
00:36:45,329 --> 00:36:47,288
- Did you get the drive?
- [speaking English] It wasn't there.

687
00:36:47,289 --> 00:36:49,916
But Reacher is still
very invested in helping us,

688
00:36:49,917 --> 00:36:51,208
so that's good.

689
00:36:51,209 --> 00:36:53,629
- Okay.
- What's all this?

690
00:36:54,129 --> 00:36:55,588
[speaking Indonesian] I'm going to
make us soto ayam.

691
00:36:55,589 --> 00:36:58,592
I can't eat any more American food.

692
00:36:59,843 --> 00:37:03,847
Perhaps this day won't turn out
so terrible after all.

693
00:37:06,892 --> 00:37:10,394
You're the officer who called in
the Mary Ellen Daniels murder, right?

694
00:37:10,395 --> 00:37:13,064
Yeah. She was with Ben Merrick
the night she died.

695
00:37:13,065 --> 00:37:14,690
It's all a part of a larger investigation.

696
00:37:14,691 --> 00:37:18,194
Well, I just received a wound analysis
you might be interested in.

697
00:37:18,195 --> 00:37:20,655
It appears Ben's throat was cut
by the same type of weapon

698
00:37:20,656 --> 00:37:21,823
Mary was killed with.

699
00:37:25,661 --> 00:37:27,663
[portentous music playing]

700
00:37:51,645 --> 00:37:54,856
Don't move and don't scream.

701
00:37:57,734 --> 00:37:59,735
Unless you want to end up like them,

702
00:37:59,736 --> 00:38:01,363
tell us where the drive is.

703
00:38:06,785 --> 00:38:08,203
Knives.

704
00:38:10,247 --> 00:38:12,124
So no one hears a gunshot.

705
00:38:15,168 --> 00:38:17,169
[medical examiner] It's a curved knife.

706
00:38:17,170 --> 00:38:18,295
It's called a karambit.

707
00:38:18,296 --> 00:38:21,508
Used by practitioners
of certain martial arts in Indonesia.

708
00:38:24,386 --> 00:38:25,804
Indonesia.

709
00:38:36,565 --> 00:38:39,275
[dramatic music playing]

710
00:38:39,276 --> 00:38:40,861
[vehicles honking]

711
00:39:02,090 --> 00:39:03,508
♪ ♪

712
00:39:19,816 --> 00:39:20,900
- [door crashes]
- [SWAT officer] Philly PD!

713
00:39:20,901 --> 00:39:23,445
Hands up! Hands up!

714
00:39:25,864 --> 00:39:28,492
Don't you fucking move.

